      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q">Is my data uploaded or stored anywhere?</div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            No. Every test runs locally in your browser. Key presses, mouse
            movement, and refresh-rate measurements never leave your device or
            get sent to any server.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q">
            Why does my 1000Hz mouse show a lower polling rate in the test?
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            This is a browser limitation, not a problem with your mouse.
            Browsers typically throttle input event updates to conserve battery
            and CPU, so a 1000Hz mouse may display 60–120Hz in-browser even
            though the hardware is reporting correctly at full rate.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q">
            Why is my keyboard stuck at 125Hz even on a gaming keyboard?
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Standard office and budget keyboards are hardware-locked to 125Hz
            regardless of software settings. Only keyboards explicitly marketed
            with 1000Hz+ polling (common in gaming keyboards) can exceed this —
            check your keyboard's spec sheet to confirm.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q">
            Are these tests actually accurate, or just an estimate?
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Browser-based tests measure input as it reaches your operating
            system — through the same path any game or app would receive it.
            This makes them highly reliable for comparison purposes, though a
            browser can't bypass OS-level polling limits. For example, most
            browsers cap visual updates around 60–120Hz, so a 1000Hz mouse may
            show a lower number even when the hardware itself is working
            correctly.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q">
            Do I need to install anything to use these tools?
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            No. All five tools run entirely in your browser using native web
            APIs — nothing is installed, and no drivers are required.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q">Do these tools work on laptops and trackpads?</div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Yes — the keyboard and mouse testers work with built-in laptop
            keyboards and trackpads as well as external peripherals, on any
            modern browser.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q">
            Which browser gives the most accurate results?
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Chromium-based browsers (Chrome, Edge) generally expose the most
            precise timing APIs for polling-rate and refresh-rate measurement.
            Firefox and Safari work fine too, but numbers can vary slightly
            between browsers due to differences in event throttling.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q">
            Can these tools detect a faulty or dying peripheral?
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Yes, indirectly. Inconsistent jitter on the mouse test, keys that
            fail to register on the keyboard tester, or a refresh rate that
            won't hold steady on the monitor test are all signs worth
            investigating further — often before assuming you need a
            replacement.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q">
            Can I check for dead pixels before buying a laptop?
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Yes — run the Screen Test's Full Health Check in a dark room at full
            brightness. It cycles through black, white, and color screens so you
            can spot dead or stuck pixels and backlight bleed in under a minute.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
